1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,425
This is a planet you can walk all the way around.

2
00:00:03,375 --> 00:00:07,600
And I didn't write most of the code — an AI agent did.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,737
I'm a web developer.

4
00:00:09,737 --> 00:00:12,375
I've never made a 3D game.

5
00:00:12,375 --> 00:00:19,800
So I gave an AI one job: build gravity that pulls you toward the center of a tiny planet, like The Little Prince.

6
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:21,675
Here's what happened.

7
00:00:21,675 --> 00:00:25,262
In a normal game, down is just... down.

8
00:00:25,262 --> 00:00:29,462
But on a tiny planet, down is different everywhere you stand.

9
00:00:29,462 --> 00:00:35,112
Your "up" has to point away from the planet's center, and it changes with every single step.

10
00:00:35,112 --> 00:00:41,700
Get this wrong, and your character either falls off into space, or walks like he's had too much to drink.

11
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:43,775
So here's the setup.

12
00:00:43,775 --> 00:00:47,537
Godot 4, one sphere, one character.

13
00:00:47,537 --> 00:00:54,925
I wrote a design doc — just a markdown file explaining exactly what I wanted — and handed it to a coding agent.

14
00:00:54,925 --> 00:00:56,862
Build me a planet.

15
00:00:56,862 --> 00:00:59,487
Make gravity point at its heart.

16
00:00:59,487 --> 00:01:04,462
First try: the agent gave me normal gravity... and a round planet.

17
00:01:04,462 --> 00:01:09,125
The character slid right off, like the whole planet was made of ice.

18
00:01:09,125 --> 00:01:13,762
Second try: he kept flipping upside down near the south pole.

19
00:01:13,762 --> 00:01:17,437
I'm not gonna lie — this part took the longest.

20
00:01:17,437 --> 00:01:20,287
The fix turned out to be one idea.

21
00:01:20,287 --> 00:01:24,987
Every frame, your "up" is the direction from the planet's center to you.

22
00:01:24,987 --> 00:01:26,987
Rotate to match it.

23
00:01:26,987 --> 00:01:29,512
Gravity always points home.

24
00:01:29,512 --> 00:01:33,587
Once the agent got that one idea — everything clicked.

25
00:01:33,587 --> 00:01:35,075
And now...

26
00:01:35,075 --> 00:01:37,562
I can walk over the horizon.

27
00:01:37,562 --> 00:01:39,525
It sounds small.

28
00:01:39,525 --> 00:01:42,862
But this is the foundation of the entire game.

29
00:01:42,862 --> 00:01:48,975
Every tree, every house, every fox I add later — all of it stands on this math.

30
00:01:48,975 --> 00:01:57,275
Next time, I'll ask the AI to scatter trees and rocks across the planet — without them floating in the air or sinking into the ground.

31
00:01:57,275 --> 00:02:02,400
Subscribe if you want to watch a web developer accidentally become a game developer.

